\alttext(Three epochs of complete compact fitting. Fitting BCE improves while unseen-class BCE worsens;one-shot validation selects a checkpoint independently of the pair threshold.)

\image(figures/siamese_episodes.png)
\caption(First eight actual held-out3-way episodes:query,then three support images in sampled order. The selected support border is green when correct and red when wrong;the adjacent table gives zero-based correct and selected columns.)
\alttext(Actual held-out MNIST queries and one support per unseen digit class,with model-selected support borders.)

The short source protocol is deliberately reported even when it fails to
improve generalization. Training-pair BCE reduction does not establish useful
unseen-class verification or one-shot transfer. Compare trained,untrained and
pixel baselines directly in the computed results;the three-way chance rate
is1/3. These are class-disjoint MNIST teaching tasks,not the paper’s Omniglot
20-way benchmark. Score calibration at threshold zero and support ranking
measure different behavior.

> Conclusion

The encoder is shared structurally and its two gradient contributions add. Stable verification derivatives and actual held-out episodes make the computation inspectable. This bounded fit can improve fitting loss while transferring worse than its untrained or pixel baselines;the computed comparisons show that limitation directly.

> References

- [Koch,Zemel and Salakhutdinov(2015),Siamese Neural Networks for One-shot Image Recognition](https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~rsalakhu/papers/oneshot1.pdf).
